Q: TcpListener: how to stop listening while awaiting AcceptTcpClientAsync()? A: <p>This is somehow an age old question that was never really answered to my satisfaction. Let's say you don't want to actually stop the server, because... You just want to wait for a client and if none connects you want to do something else (like cleaning up no longer connected client threads for example). Coming from the C and Winsock-World this seemed a lot like a non-blocking socket/poll problem to me.</p>
<p>Consider the following code, which is an extension function to TcpListener:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static class TcpListenerExtensions
{
    // Cancellable function polling the server socket for new connections
    public static async Task&lt;TcpClient&gt; AcceptTcpClientAsync(this TcpListener listener, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        // Save old blocking state
        Boolean wasBlocking = listener.Server.Blocking;

        // try/finally to reset blocking mode to original state
        try
        {
            // Enter non-blocking mode
            listener.Server.Blocking = false;

            while (!ct.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Wait for up to 100ms for a client
                var clientAvailable = listener.Server.Poll(100 * 1000, SelectMode.SelectRead);

                // If no client available, check the cancellation token and wait again
                if (!clientAvailable) continue;

                try
                {
                    // Try to accept the client
                    var acceptedClient = await listener.AcceptTcpClientAsync();

                    // Success, return the client.
                    return acceptedClient;
                }
                catch (SocketException e) when (e.SocketErrorCode == SocketError.WouldBlock)
                {
                    // Whoops, the client has been dropped before we were able to accept it
                    // Nevermind, continue waiting loop.
                    continue;
                }
            }

            // We were cancelled, before we were able to accept a client.
            // Throw Operation Cancelled Exception
            throw new OperationCanceledException(ct);
        }
        finally
        {
            listener.Server.Blocking = wasBlocking;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>What this does is the following:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Set the server socket into non-blocking mode (see why in a later step).</p>
</li>
<li><p>While no cancellation is requested...</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Use <code>poll()</code> to wait for 100ms (adjustable) on the server socket for a client to connect to it.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If <em>no</em> client is present according to <code>poll()</code>, start the next loop iteration.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If a client <em>is</em> present according to <code>poll()</code>, use <code>AcceptTcpClientAsync()</code> to accept it.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If the client is still here, this returns a TcpClient object. We just return this and the function finishes.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If the client dropped out somewhere between <code>poll()</code> and <code>AcceptTcpClientAsync()</code> for some reason, this throws a SocketException with EWOULDBLOCK, <strong>because the server socket is in non-blocking mode</strong>. Otherwise it would block here until another client connects. This way, we can just ignore this exception and wait for another client.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Once cancellation is requested (and we only see this because we got no client connection in time), we throw an Operation Cancelled Exception.</p>
</li>
<li><p>On a side-note: We use a try/finally to restore the blocking mode to its original value.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Usage is as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Example Token Source, cancelling after 10 seconds
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
cts.CancelAfter(10000);

// Start a new listener
var listener = new TcpListener(IPAddress.Any, 1025);
listener.Start();

try
{
    // Wait for a client
    var client = await listener.AcceptTcpClientAsync(cts.Token);

    Console.WriteLine(&quot;Client connected.&quot;);
    // Do something with the client here...
    client.Close();
}
catch (OperationCanceledException)
{
    // The timeout of the cancellation token source fired
    Console.WriteLine(&quot;No client connected.&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>So while this approach uses a loop polling the cancellation token, 100ms of delay (jitter, really) for the shutdown in a cancellation case are most probably fine for any application waiting for TCP client connections. And if not, the timeout of <code>poll()</code> can always be adapted. Aside from this fact, this solution ticks all boxes for me. Enjoy.</p>

Q: JSON parse array of data A: <p>I think this is what you're after:</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>foreach my $item (@{$data}) {
    foreach my $key (keys %{$item}) {
        print &quot;$key: $item-&gt;{$key}\n&quot;;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>foreach my $item (@{$data}) {
    while (my ($key, $val) = each %{$item}) {
        print &quot;$key: $val\n&quot;;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Possible output:</p>
<pre><code>sent: 2026-01-16T17:00:00Z
data: ARRAY(0x55c9993005f0)
data: ARRAY(0x55c999308938)
sent: 2026-01-16T17:00:00Z
</code></pre>
<p>Note: I had to fix a syntax error too. You didn't end the <code>$json_text</code> definition with <code>;</code>.</p>

Q: How to check if the user is visiting the site&#39;s root url? A: <p>In modern browsers you can use URLPattern, e.g.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const rootPattern = new URLPattern({ pathname: '/' });
const isRoot = rootPattern.test(window.location.href);
</code></pre>
<p>By using URLPattern the query params are ignored.</p>

Q: Javascript map method on array of string elements A: <p><strong>Javascript map method on array of string elements by using split() function.</strong></p>
<pre><code>let str = 'hello';

    str.split('').map((x)=&gt; {
        console.log(&quot;|&quot;+x+&quot;|&quot;);
        return x;
    })
</code></pre>
